> tl;dr
>
> - Split sitemap‑like content out of the global footer; created a separate, scannable directory.
> - Clarified orientation questions in the footer; improved findability via the directory.
> - Result of collaboration across content, IA and engineering teams under strong facilitative leadership.

EMBL.org’s “footmap” had become a screen‑and‑a‑half of links. The fix wasn’t a bigger footer—it was reframing roles. The footer now answers orientation and contact needs; the directory provides a compact map of major areas.

## Problem

Users arrived at the bottom of pages to find dense, repetitive navigation that didn’t help with core footer tasks (What is EMBL? How to contact? Where next?).

## Approach

- Define distinct jobs for the footer vs directory; remove directory bloat from footer.
- Design a fast, scannable directory page; keep footer focused on missions and contact.
- Apply VF patterns so improvements can propagate across properties.

## Key decisions

- Footer handles orientation (about, contact, social) not site mapping.
- Directory becomes the maintained map for major areas; no more link dumps in footer.

## Outcomes

- Cleaner page bottoms; faster orientation and fewer competing calls to action.
- Directory usage provides clearer signals about wayfinding and content gaps.

